      "text": "Here's a solid overview to help you get started on your paper!\n\n---\n\n## *Project Hail Mary* by Andy Weir — Background & Reception\n\n### Publication Details\n- **Published:** 2021\n- **Publisher:** Ballantine Books\n- **Format:** 476 pages (~117,000 words), available in hardcover, paperback, eBook, and audiobook (narrated by Ray Porter on Audible)\n- **It is Andy Weir's third novel**, following *The Martian* (2011) and *Artemis* (2017).\n\n### Plot Overview\nThe novel follows **Ryland Grace**, a middle-school science teacher who wakes up alone on a spacecraft with no memory of how he got there. He gradually recalls that he is humanity's last hope — sent on a solo mission to investigate **Astrophage**, a microorganism that is dimming the Sun and threatening Earth with extinction. Deep in space, he makes an extraordinary discovery: an alien named **Rocky** from the Eridian system, whose civilization faces the same crisis. The two form an unlikely partnership to solve the problem together.\n\n### Key Themes\n- **Science and problem-solving** (hallmark of Weir's style)\n- **First contact and communication**\n- **Friendship and sacrifice**\n- **Survival against impossible odds**\n\n### Critical & Reader Reception\n- Named **one of the best science fiction novels of 2021** by multiple publications.\n- Holds an exceptionally high **reader rating of 4.8/5** across nearly 48,000 ratings — one of the highest-rated recent sci-fi novels.\n- Critics and readers consistently praise its **optimistic tone, scientific rigor, the protagonist's compelling voice**, and especially the central relationship between Grace and Rocky, which many call one of **the most heartwarming in the genre**.\n- Frequently compared to **Weir's own *The Martian*** and the classic ***Rendezvous with Rama*** by Arthur C. Clarke.\n\n### Adaptations\nA **film adaptation** is in development at **Amazon MGM Studios**, with **Ryan Gosling** attached to star as Ryland Grace.\n\n---\n\nThis should give you a strong foundation for your paper! Would you like me to dig deeper into any particular aspect — such as its scientific themes, its place in the first-contact subgenre, or comparisons to other works?",
